# Child-facing UI implementation

Applies when editing presentation layers for the **child play experience** (not parent/admin tools unless noted). For the shared mascot, use [little-adventure-character.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-character.mdc) (`LittleAdventureCharacter`); do not add per-game character SVGs or duplicate companion components. For environment layers and tappable props, follow [little-adventure-world.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-world.mdc) (touch targets, hierarchy, sensory-friendly tap feedback).

## World vs UI

- **Illustrated environments** belong to the **page** (full-bleed or natural band), not a nested “artwork card” with heavy frames or card-within-card chrome.
- Learning cards, TODAY, weekday strip, and controls stay **existing React/UI** — do not bake them into environment artwork.
- Prefer **illustrated environment assets** when CSS/SVG primitives would make the character feel pasted onto a diagram; use CSS/SVG only for simple supporting layout/blend when quality stays coherent.
- **Illustrated child-facing UI** (e.g. WTW day/event banner frames) may ship as raster assets under `public/environments/ui/` when visual coherence requires it; keep dynamic text and interaction glow in React/CSS, not baked into the PNG.

### Walk Through the Week

- **One illustrated world** — environment is the dominant canvas; Today/weekday strip and storybook banner **overlay** the sky/world (not a separate column above a small artwork strip).
- **Today** and the **weekday strip** sit in the **upper/sky** area; lightweight, calm, not a large card.
- Active day/event: **storybook banner** (cream/paper plaque, organic shape, large day typography) — not a white **`ChildCard`** lesson panel on illustrated-world screens (Monday → School first). The banner is the **forward tap target** on the WTW day screen (no separate Next). Locked while voice plays; ready after “Tap to move forward” with a **soft gold glow** and slow breathe (not pink/green/neon card chrome).
- Banner architecture: illustrated frame at `public/environments/walk-through-week/day-event-banner.png` + dynamic day/event from data (School, Chai Shop, Cake, Park); see world rules §16. Same banner artwork across events; interaction glow in CSS, not in the PNG.
- Entire visible banner is the touch target (~60–80px minimum); do not add a giant invisible hit rectangle or a floating extra button.
- Environment: **natural aspect ratio**, scale to dominate; avoid `object-cover` crops that hide path/destination. Character ~25–30% of scene height, **standing on the path**.
- Composition reference: `docs/design-references/walk-through-week/school-day-reference.png` (design only).

## Touch and layout

- Minimum **60px** effective touch target for tappable controls (button, draggable, choice tile). Use padding and `min-width` / `min-height` or expanded hit slop — not just visible graphic size.
- Spacing between tappable items: enough gap that mis-taps are rare (generous margins on tablet).
- Avoid `:hover`-only affordances; every action must work with tap alone.
- Prefer full-screen or large regions over dense control bars.

## Feedback

- **Correct / complete**: brief celebratory motion (scale, sparkle, bounce), optional positive sound; avoid long modal text.
- **Incorrect / incomplete**: soft wiggle, gentle dim, or “nudge” animation; invite retry without copy when possible. No red flash, shake punishment, or error toasts with sentences.
- Animations: short duration, ease-out; no rapid strobing or many simultaneous effects.

## Content

- Do not add scores, countdown timers, lives, or “level failed” states in child UI unless product explicitly asks for a separate competitive mode (default: off).
- Prefer pictorial choices over numbered lists or paragraphs.
- Loading: friendly idle animation or character; avoid “Loading…” paragraphs.

## Accessibility without breaking “game feel”

- Support reduced motion when available; keep essential feedback visible without relying on motion alone.
- If text exists for screen readers, keep on-screen visuals primary for sighted children.
